      "thinking": "The real fault is that the deploy pushed a bad port, and the code just keeps retrying the connection on every request, paying full latency with no memory that the cache is down. The resilient fix is adding a circuit breaker so after a failure it stops hammering the cache for a while instead of blowing up latency \u2014 config validation could help too, but the circuit breaker is the sturdier fix.\n\nI'm looking at how the `look_up` seam is structured, since the connection attempt lives inside it \u2014 the module could short-circuit by skipping the call if the cache was recently found unreachable. I want to check whether pricing_service or payment_provider already has an established breaker/slow-call pattern I should imitate, since a module-level global breaker feels ugly, and the caller who holds the seam lives outside this repo, which complicates keeping state across requests. Since account_page calls cached_summary without any breaker state, I want to look at pricing_service's handling of repeated failures to see if there's a reusable pattern.\n\n",
